            <ArticleTitle>In the United States, &quot;Opt-Out&quot; States Show No Increase in Access to Anesthesia Services for Medicare Beneficiaries Compared with Non-&quot;Opt-Out&quot; States.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>283-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1213/XAA.0000000000000293</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In the United States, anesthesia care can be provided by anesthesiologists or nurse anesthetists. Since 2001, 17 states have exercised their right to &quot;opt-out&quot; of the federal requirement that a physician supervise the administration of anesthesia by a nurse anesthetist, with the majority citing increased access to anesthesia care as the rationale for their decision. By using Medicare data, we found that most (4 of 5) cohorts of &quot;opt-out&quot; states likely experienced smaller growth in anesthesia utilization rates compared with non-&quot;opt-out&quot; states, suggesting that opt-out was not associated with an increase in access to anesthesia care.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
